(*
MethodProfileNodeC6 — the #164 device measurement: what does an exact per-method
call counter cost on a C6, in the shadow of a real interpreter?

WHY HANOI. `hanoiMove:` makes exactly 2^n - 1 activations, so the workload
predicts its own activation count and the counter can be checked against a
number rather than against itself. Body verbatim from HanoiNode.nsl (which took
it from 00_perf/performance.nsl), self-timed via `millis` the same way.

WHY 20 IDENTICAL RUNS AND NOT TWO BUILDS. A cross-binary OFF/ON comparison of
this hook on host put the ON build 4% FASTER — code-layout roulette, not a
result. So both arms live in ONE binary, one boot, one cache state, and the VM
flips the counter on at a fixed ACTIVATION COUNT (-DOEVM_MPROF_FLIP_AT). Runs
1-10 are the OFF arm, runs 12-20 the ON arm, and run 11 is discarded because the
flip lands inside it.

  depth 18   -> 262143 activations per run
  20 runs    -> 5242860, so FLIP_AT=2621430 (mid-run-11) and DUMP_AT=5242860

Build (no Thread needed — this workload never touches the network):
  OEVM_METHOD_PROFILE=1 OEVM_MPROF_RUNTIME_GATE=1 \
  OEVM_MPROF_FLIP_AT=2621430u OEVM_MPROF_DUMP_AT=5242860u \
  ./run.sh --board esp32c6 --node MethodProfileNodeC6.nsl --flash
*)
class MethodProfileNodeC6 packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | i |
    ('mprof-node: depth 18, 20 runs, 262143 activations each') out.
    i:: 1.
    [ i <= 20 ] whileTrue: [
      self runHanoi: 18 index: i.
      i:: i + 1.
    ].
    ('mprof-node: done') out.
    ^ 0
  )
  runHanoi: n index: i = (
    | t0 t1 |
    t0:: 0 millis.
    self hanoiMove: n from: 1 to: 2 temp: 3.
    t1:: 0 millis.
    ('mprof-run ' , i printString , ' ' , (t1 - t0) printString) out.
    ^ n
  )
  public hanoiMove: n from: src to: dst temp: tmp = (
    n > 1 ifTrue: [
      self hanoiMove: n - 1 from: src to: tmp temp: dst.
      self hanoiMove: n - 1 from: tmp to: dst temp: src.
    ].
    ^ n
  )
) : (
)
